A linguagem escolhida foi RUST


fonte da gramática consultada: https://web.mit.edu/rust-lang_v1.25/arch/amd64_ubuntu1404/share/doc/rust/html/grammar.html



código que será derivado: 
fn main() {
    for i in 1..=10 {
        println!("{}", i);
    }
}

derivação passo a passo;

1. <item> → <function>
2. <function> → fn <ident> ( <params>? ) <block>
3. <block> → { <statements>? }
4. <statements> → <statement>
5. <statement> → <expression_statement>
6. <expression_statement> → <expression> ;
7. <expression> → <for_expression>
8. <for_expression> → for <pattern> in <expression> <block>
9. <pattern> → <ident>
10. <ident> → i
11. <expression> → <range_expression>
12. <range_expression> → <range_expr>
13. <range_expr> → <expression> .. <expression>
14. <expression> → <literal>
15. <literal> → 1
16. <literal> → 11
17. <expression_statement> → <macro_invocation> ;
18. <macro_invocation> → <ident> ! ( <token_tree> )


simbolos terminais e não terminais:
Símbolos não terminais: LoopExpression, Pattern, Expression, RangeExpression, BlockExpression, Statements e Statement. Esses símbolos representam categorias sintáticas da gramática e podem ser substituídos por outras produções.
Símbolos terminais: for, i, in, 1, ..=, 10, {, }, println!, (, ), "{}" e ;. Esses símbolos correspondem aos elementos que aparecem na sequência final do código.



Regras:

1. <item> → <function>
2. <function> → fn <ident> ( <params>? ) <block>
3. <block> → { <statements>? }
4. <statements> → <statement>
5. <statement> → <expression_statement>
6. <expression_statement> → <expression> ;
7. <expression> → <for_expression>
8. <for_expression> → for <pattern> in <expression> <block>
9. <pattern> → <ident>
10. <ident> → i
11. <expression> → <range_expression>
12. <range_expression> → <range_expr>
13. <range_expr> → <expression> .. <expression>
14. <expression> → <literal>
15. <literal> → 1
16. <literal> → 11
17. <expression_statement> → <macro_invocation> ;
18. <macro_invocation> → <ident> ! ( <token_tree> )

